  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1148 PM CDT SUN SEP 6 2026\n\nIsolated showers and storms remain possible (10 to 20%) through\n4 AM, primarily across the southeast. Given the earlier rainfall,\npatchy fog is anticipated across the east through sunrise.\n\nFor the upcoming day, surface flow becomes easterly, resulting in\na slight drop in temperatures and dew points. Drier air aloft\nmoving in from the north will limit convective potential across\nthe northern third of the (CWA), while scattered storms remain in\nthe forecast south of I-20. Afternoon surface dew points are \nexpected to mix into the upper 60s to lower 70s, keeping heat \nindices just below Heat Advisory criteria. While no advisory is \nanticipated for Monday, heat indices will rise to 100-105 degrees.\nThe easterly surface flow will strengthen as the pressure \ngradient tightens near the surface ridge over the Appalachians. \nExpect wind gusts near 20 mph, providing heat relief for eastern \nand northeastern counties.\n\nThe 500 mb ridge is expected to amplify by midweek. High \nconfidence exists that rising heights and drier air aloft will \nlimit rain chances to the I-85 corridor and points south through \nWednesday. Meanwhile, global deterministic guidance maintains \nconsistency in moving an upper-level low westward over the \nnorthern Gulf by Wednesday and Thursday, transporting additional \ntropical moisture northward. Consequently, diurnal shower and \nstorm chances are expected to increase, with highs reaching the \nlow to mid-90s. By the end of the week, an upper trough will dive \ntoward the Great Lakes. A surface cold front will approach the \nregion by Friday and Saturday; however, current guidance indicates\nlimited upper-level support for the front to penetrate as far \nsouth as Central Alabama.\n\n16",
